{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1452545-aberrant-transformation-and-transcendence","id":1452545,"title":"[Aberrant] Transformation and Transcendence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-16T15:15:28-07:00","id":1452545,"title":"[Aberrant] Transformation and Transcendence","content":"After rereading the character creation chapter it seems that both are meant to be included. From what I can see Transformation drops the XP cost for that trait by 3 or six. So if you take a moderate transformation you could, for example, buy Quantum at 10xp/dot. This cost break continues in play. The cost break does not apply to related mega-edges (for example techniques) unless you take it specifically for that group of edges (for example taking a transformation that only drops the cost to buy techniques for a given power). Since power tags are also defined as traits, the cost break for the power does not include a cost break for buying extra trags. Transformation are a good buy for Nova traits that are going to be taken to 4 or 5 in character creation.<br /> <br /> Transcendence on the other hand, sets the cost for a certain dot to 6. This can mean a &quot;free&quot; dot if taken as part of a transformation. Basically, transcendence gives you six more XP. (This might reduce the XP for a power suite, but simply assign the transcendence to the technique mega edge.<br /> <br /> Finally, negative tags completely remove the cost of a tag (12 xp). They can also remove the cost of a dot, though a minimum cost of 6xp remains. These are best used when you want a one point mega-edge for half cost (for example dread presence only working when a character is transformed) or are conceptually limiting a power, perhaps by using restricted power 3 to turn transformation into a single form &quot;war form.&quot; Unlike the other options, negative tags effect the cost of power, meaning that they reduce the XP in a power suite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25593-drsteve","name":"DrSteve","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T20:30:19-07:00","id":1452734,"title":"","content":"I am pretty sure that transformations and transcendence essentially work the same way. The best way to think about either is that the power or trait has the full cost, but you get a refund of 3 or 6 XP. I am pretty sure that each essentially only counts once, not once per dot of trait. <br /> <br /> so for your quantum example, you could buy one point of quantum for 10XP, but any others would still cost 16. <br /> <br /> if you take a power with a transformation, and the a technique mega edge for that power, treat the power as if you had paid the full cost when constructing your technique. <br /> <br /> basically both are a way to boost your character a bit for some cost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24929-econclark","name":"econclark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}